From 3d0b8b73b2d411f7e9d3b93cbe4d53d01624475c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bakhodirovxz <bohodirovasadbek1@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 02:25:28 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 7/8] calc: one dependency-graph entry per run of shared
 formulas

The graph kept, for every formula, a node with its precedent areas and an entry in a hash set per
precedent cell: about 326 bytes per formula, a quarter of the peak memory on a large workbook.

Consecutive formulas down a column that are shifted copies of one tree at the same offset now form
a run: one node whose precedent areas move with the row (each end of an area is a constant row or
the formula's row plus a constant), indexed by the rows the whole run reads. `dependents` and
`dependents_of_range` turn a hit into the rows of the run that read the cell, so dirty marking and
recalculation see exactly the formulas they saw before. A run is only formed when its first, last
and middle formulas give those areas through `precedents` (no dynamic references, no area that
turns over inside the run, no reference off the sheet); otherwise its formulas are nodes as before.
Editing a formula of a run splits the run.

The new tests compare every query of the graph with runs against the graph with one node per
formula, before and after edits.

Open peak on the 10 MB generated workbook: 352 to 194 MB (40 MB: 1,390 to 751 MB; 80 MB: 2,771 to
1,496 MB), with every computed value identical.
